        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the lightest treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Of the machines we could confirm, the UREVO Strol 2E Pro is the lightest motorized treadmill at 44 lb by its Amazon listing, and Sunny's foldable manual walking treadmill is 46.9 lb by Sunny's figure. Some pads claim to be lighter but give no unit weight in their listings.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a lightweight treadmill weigh?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Walking pads and manual treadmills typically weigh about 35 to 60 lb, 2-in-1 pads with a running mode about 45 to 80 lb, compact folding treadmills about 90 to 130 lb, and full folding treadmills 150 lb or more. The machines on this page range from 44 to 66.1 lb.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can one person move a lightweight treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              On a hard floor, yes: tilt it onto its front wheels and roll it. Stairs are different. Even a 44 lb machine is long and awkward, with its weight at the motor end, so two people is the safe way to carry one up or down a flight.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can you run on a lightweight treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              You can jog on some. The Sunny Treadpad Compact reaches 6 mph, the UREVO Strol 2E Pro 6.2 mph and the WalkingPad Z1R 5 mph, but their belts are 42 to 48 inches long against 55 to 60 inches on running treadmills. For regular running, a heavier folding treadmill is the better tool.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a lightweight folding treadmill less durable?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not necessarily, but it has less margin. Light machines tend to have lower weight limits and smaller motors designed around walking. Staying well under the stated limit, keeping to the intended speeds and lubricating the belt as the manual says matter more on a light machine than a heavy one.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
